---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfEditRecolorPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf/PdfEditRecolorPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="invert-pdf-colors">
  <PdfEditRecolorPdf client:load mode="invert" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>A dark copy that stays dark everywhere</h2>
      <p>
        Many PDF readers have a night mode, but it works only in that reader. Send the file to a
        colleague, open it on an e-reader or a phone, or project it in a dark lecture theatre, and the
        page is white again.
      </p>
      <p>
        To invert the PDF colors in the file makes the change permanent and portable. The document is
        dark in all viewers, on all devices, with no setting to find.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Dark mode versus a true negative</h2>
      <p>
        These give very different results.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="min-w-[640px]">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Input colour</th>
              <th scope="col">Dark mode (lightness flip)</th>
              <th scope="col">True negative (channel inversion)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>White paper</td>
              <td>Black</td>
              <td>Black</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Black text</td>
              <td>White</td>
              <td>White</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Red stamp</td>
              <td>Red</td>
              <td>Cyan</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Blue heading</td>
              <td>Blue</td>
              <td>Orange</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Mid grey</td>
              <td>Mid grey</td>
              <td>Mid grey</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Dark mode changes each pixel's lightness and keeps hue and saturation. For a channel value
        <code>c</code>, with the pixel's brightest and darkest channels <code>max</code> and
        <code>min</code>, the new value is <code>255 − max − min + c</code>. The order of the three
        channels, which your eye reads as hue, stays the same, and the lightest becomes the darkest. A
        true negative computes <code>255 − c</code> per channel, which also changes the hue to its
        complement.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use dark mode for reading. Use the true negative when you want a negative: to read a scanned
        film strip, recover a photograph of a chalkboard, or inspect a lithography mask.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Battery and eye comfort</h2>
      <p>
        On an OLED or AMOLED screen (most modern phones, many tablets, some laptops), black pixels are
        off. A page of text is mostly background, so an inverted document cuts panel power, often by a
        lot at high brightness.
      </p>
      <p>
        On an LCD the backlight stays on, so there is no power saving. The benefit there is comfort:
        less light in a dark room, which most people find easier over a long reading session. Neither
        effect is a health claim. In normal daylight, the evidence on reading speed and eye strain
        favours dark text on a light background. Invert for night reading, not as a default.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What inversion costs</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The text layer: to recolour, the tool renders each page, so the output is images. Nothing
          is selectable, searchable or screen-reader accessible, and links stop working. Keep the
          original.
        </li>
        <li>
          Photographs: inverting a page inverts the pictures on it. Dark mode keeps hues, so it looks
          better than a true negative, but a page that is mostly photographs is usually better as it
          is.
        </li>
        <li>
          File size: rendered pages are often larger than a compact vector original. The result panel
          shows how much changed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Printing: do not print it. A fully inverted page uses a very large amount of toner and looks
          bad. This is a format for screen reading.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Practical settings</h2>
      <p>
        For reading on a phone or tablet, 110 or 150 DPI at 82% quality gives a compact file that
        looks sharp on a high-density display. For a large-format technical drawing that you will zoom
        into, use 200 or 300 DPI and accept the size. Hold the compare button to show the original.
        Switch between the two to see if dark mode or a true negative suits the document.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Related</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/grayscale-pdf">PDF to Grayscale</a> uses the same rendering pipeline and is for cheap
        mono printing, with a true 1-bit mode. <a href="/pdf-to-png">PDF to PNG</a> exports pages as
        image files, to invert them in an image editor. <a href="/compress-pdf">Compress PDF</a> gives
        a smaller file and keeps its text.
      </p>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
